t are part of potential chows are T1, T4, T6, T8, T9, B1, B3, B4. Discarding one of these might be worse because they could be part of future chows. The honor tiles (F1, F3, J1, J3) are not part of any chows, so discarding one of them is safer.\n\nSo between F1, F3, J1, J3. Let's see which one is better to discard. Since the user's seat wind is West Wind, and the prevailing wind is East, maybe having a pung of West Wind would give them a Seat Wind pung (2 points). But they don't have any West Wind tiles. So not applicable here.\n\nIf they discard F1, they lose a possible pung of East Wind (if they can get two more). But they have only one F1. Similarly for F3 (South Wind). J1 (Red Dragon) and J3 (White Dragon). Discarding any of these might not affect high-point patterns much.\n\nSo the best choice is to discard one of the honor tiles. Let's pick one. Maybe F1, since they have only one, and there are four left in the unshown tiles. Discarding F1 would leave three in the wall, so it's safe.\n\nTherefore, the best action is to discard F1. That way, they keep their potential for forming chows with the other tiles and don't lose a pair or a potential pung.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation: The player has a concealed hand with a W7 pair and mixed tiles. Discarding an honor tile minimizes the risk of breaking potential melds. F1 is a safe choice as it's not part of any chow and has multiple remaining in the wall.\nReason: Discarding F1 preserves the possibility of forming chows in the T or B suits while avoiding loss of a pair or key meld components. This maintains flexibility for future draws.\nAnswer: Play F1"}
