 of 1-2-3, but they don't have T2 or T3. T4 could be part of 4-5-6, but no T5. T6 could be part of 6-7-8, but no T7. T8 and T9 could be part of 7-8-9, but no T7. So not helpful. In B suit: B1, B3, B4, B7. B1-2-3 (missing B2), B3-4-5 (missing B5), B7-8-9 (missing B8, B9). Not helpful. W suit: only W7. So All Chows is not possible.\n\nAnother possibility is All Pungs. They have two W7s, but need four Pungs. Not likely.\n\nLooking at the hand, maybe they can form a hand with All Simples, but they have T1, T9, B1, which are terminals. So that's not possible.\n\nWait, maybe they can form a hand with All Chows and a pair. Let's see. Suppose they discard a tile that allows them to form some Chows. For example, if they discard T1, maybe they can form T4-5-6 (but no T5), or T6-7-8 (no T7). Not helpful. If they discard T6, then they have T4, T8, T9. Still not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they can form some Pungs. They have two W7s. If they draw another W7, that's a Pung. But there are four W7s left. So possible. But they need to discard a tile to make room for drawing.\n\nAnother angle: since they have no melds, they're going for a concealed hand. Concealed hand gives 2 points. If they can form other patterns, that's better.\n\nLooking at their tiles, they have two W7s (a pair), and other singles. If they can form four Chows and a pair, that's All Chows (24 points). But as discussed, that's not possible with current tiles. Alternatively, maybe All Simples, but they have terminals and honors.\n\nWait, the user has a T1, which is a terminal, and B1, which is a terminal. So All Simples is out. All Simples requires no terminals or honors.\n\nAnother pattern is Fully Concealed Hand (4 points). If they can form a hand where all melds are concealed, and the winning tile is drawn. But they need to have four melds and a pair. Since they have no melds yet, they need to form them by drawing. For example, if they discard a tile, then draw a tile to form a Pung or Chows.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they can look for a way to form a hand with All Chows. Let's think again. Suppose they discard T1. Then they have T4, T6, T8, T9, B1, B3, B4, B7. If they can form a Chows with T4-5-6 (but no T5), T6-7-8 (no T7), T8-9-10 (invalid). Not helpful. If they discard T6, then they have T4, T8, T9, B1, B3, B4, B7. Still not helpful.\n\nWhat about discarding B1? Then they have T1, T4, T6, T8, T9, B3, B4, B7. Still no Chows. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they can form a hand with All Pungs. But they have two W7s. If they draw another W7, that's a Pung. Then they need three more Pungs. Not possible with current tiles.\n\nWait, maybe they can form a hand with All Pungs and a pair. But they need four Pungs. They have two W7s. If they get two more W7s, that's a Kong. But that would be a Kong, not a Pung. So maybe not.\n\nAnother thought: since they have a pair of W7s, maybe they can use that as the pair, and the rest as four melds. But the rest are all singles. So they need to form four melds (either Chows or Pungs) with the remaining 11 tiles. That's not possible unless they draw a tile to complete a Pung or Chows.\n\nFor example, if they discard a tile, then draw a tile to complete a Pung. Let's say they discard T1. Then they have 12 tiles left. If they draw another W7, they can form a Pung of W7s. Then they have three W7s (a Pung), and the remaining 9 tiles need to form three more melds. But the remaining tiles are T4, T6, T8, T9, B1, B3, B4, B7, F3, J1, J3. Not sure how to form three more melds.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they can form some Chows. For example, if they have T4, T6, T8. Maybe T4-5-6 (no T5), T6-7-8 (no T7), T8-9-10 (invalid). Not helpful. Similarly for B3, B4, B7. Maybe B3-4-5 (no B5), B7-8-9 (no B8, B9). Not helpful.\n\nThis seems challenging. Maybe the best approach is to discard a tile that is least useful for forming melds, to preserve the possibility of drawing a useful tile. Let's look at the tiles they can discard:\n\nPossible discards: B3, B7, T8,