e user also has a pair of W7s. If they can form a pung of W7s, that's worth points. However, since they only have two, they need another W7. The unshown tiles have 4 W7s left, so it's possible. But if they discard a W7 now, they lose the chance to form a pung. \n\nWait, the user has two W7s in their hand. The valid action includes Play W7. If they discard a W7, they might be giving up the chance to form a pung. But if they keep it, maybe they can form a pung later. However, since the user is trying to maximize their score, keeping the W7s might be better for forming a pung or kong. \n\nSo the user should avoid discarding W7 unless necessary. \n\nLooking at the other tiles, the user has B1 (terminal), which is a 1 in Dots. B7 is a 7 in Dots (non-terminal). So if the user is going for All Terminals and Honors, they need to discard B7. \n\nSo between the options, playing B7 would be a good move because it's a non-terminal tile in Dots. That would leave the user with B1, which is a terminal. \n\nSimilarly, playing T8 (a middle tile in Characters) would help if they are aiming for All Terminals and Honors. \n\nBut the user also has T1 and T9, which are terminals. If they can keep those, it's better. \n\nAnother consideration is the possibility of forming a Seven Pairs hand. The user has two W7s. If they can form pairs in other tiles, like T1, T4, T6, T8, T9, B1, B2, B3, B4, B7, J1, J3. But they need seven pairs. Since they have 13 tiles, they need to form seven pairs with one tile left. So they need to discard a tile that's not part of a pair. \n\nIf they discard B7, which is a single tile, they can then have the remaining tiles as: two W7s, and one each of T1, T4, T6, T8, T9, B1, B2, B3, B4, J1, J3. Then, they need to form six more pairs. For example, if they can pair T1, T4, T6, T8, T9, B1, B2, B3, B4, J1, J3. But they need six pairs, so they need to have six of these tiles in pairs. \n\nBut with only one of each, they can't form pairs unless they draw another. So this